Read path is the most performance-sensitive code path; clean separation lets us swap implementations.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class TimelineController {
        +GET getHomeTimeline(userId, cursor)
        +GET getProfileTimeline(userId, cursor)
    }
    class FeedService {
        +buildHomeTimeline(userId, cursor) Page~FeedItem~
    }
    class TimelineStore {
        <<interface>>
        +readPushed(userId, limit) List~PostRef~
        +pushToFollowers(post, followerIds)
    }
    class RedisTimelineStore
    class CelebrityCache {
        +recentPosts(authorId, limit) List~PostRef~
    }
    class GraphService {
        +followeesOf(userId) List~UserId~
        +celebrityFolloweesOf(userId) List~UserId~
    }
    class CandidateMerger {
        +merge(pushed, pulled) List~PostRef~
    }
    class Ranker {
        +score(userId, candidates) List~Ranked~
    }
    class HydrationService {
        +hydrate(userId, refs) List~FeedItem~
    }
    class FanOutService {
        +onPostCreated(post)
    }
    class FanOutPolicy {
        +decide(authorFollowerCount) Strategy
    }

    TimelineController --> FeedService
    FeedService --> TimelineStore
    FeedService --> CelebrityCache
    FeedService --> GraphService
    FeedService --> CandidateMerger
    FeedService --> Ranker
    FeedService --> HydrationService
    TimelineStore <|.. RedisTimelineStore
    FanOutService --> FanOutPolicy
    FanOutService --> TimelineStore
    FanOutService --> CelebrityCache
    FanOutService --> GraphService
```

**Key code** (interesting bodies only):

```java
public class FeedService {
    public Page<FeedItem> buildHomeTimeline(UserId userId, Cursor cursor) {
        List<PostRef> pushed = timelineStore.readPushed(userId, 200);
        List<UserId> celebs = graph.celebrityFolloweesOf(userId);

        List<PostRef> pulled = celebs.parallelStream()
            .flatMap(c -> celebrityCache.recentPosts(c, 20).stream())
            .toList();

        List<PostRef> candidates = merger.merge(pushed, pulled);   // dedupe
        List<Ranked> ranked = ranker.score(userId, candidates);
        List<Ranked> page = applyCursor(ranked, cursor, 20);

        return new Page<>(hydration.hydrate(userId, page), nextCursor(page));
    }
}

public class FanOutService {
    public void onPostCreated(Post post) {
        Strategy strategy = policy.decide(post.authorFollowerCount());
        switch (strategy) {
            case PUSH_TO_FOLLOWERS -> {
                int batchSize = 1000;
                graph.followersOf(post.authorId(), batchSize)
                    .forEach(batch -> timelineStore.pushToFollowers(post, batch));
            }
            case CELEBRITY_PULL -> {
                celebrityCache.add(post.authorId(), post.id());
            }
        }
    }
}

public class FanOutPolicy {
    public Strategy decide(long followerCount) {
        return followerCount < 10_000 ? Strategy.PUSH_TO_FOLLOWERS : Strategy.CELEBRITY_PULL;
    }
}
```

Why this shape:

- **`TimelineStore` interface** — we may move from Redis ZSETs to a dedicated timeline DB (Manhattan at Twitter); the FeedService is unaware.
- **`Ranker` is its own collaborator** — lets us A/B-test ranking algorithms, run shadow traffic with two rankers, and swap to a new ML model with config.
- **`FanOutPolicy` extracted** — the threshold and tier logic live in one tested class; we tune the threshold without touching the worker.
- **Parallel celebrity fetch** — the read budget is tight; serial calls to Redis would cost 100 × 1ms = 100ms; parallel completes in ~5ms.
